{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_972377311#5_1799881130","relevance":3,"reason":"Dedicated article segment directly explains the sensation of being watched, citing Titchener and stating it arises from nervousness about what is happening behind our backs and from belief/attribution rather than paranormal perception.","learned":"Titchener's explanation for the sensation of being watched was that people are generally nervous about what is happening behind their backs, so the feeling comes from our own beliefs/attributions rather than actual extrasensory observation."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"sense someone staring\" (+ \"gaze detection\" brain threat))","reason":"Search for dedicated mainstream explanations of the common feeling that someone is staring, especially gaze-detection and threat-monitoring mechanisms."}}
