{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_1106539267#16_2468525798","relevance":0,"reason":"Psychic-medium/paranormal article, not a trustworthy psychological or scientific explanation of why people feel watched.","learned":"Queries with 'feel like someone is watching' can retrieve paranormal/spiritual interpretations unless clinical or scientific terms are made central."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"why do i feel like\" \"being watched\" (+ paranoia anxiety hypervigilance))","reason":"Use the exact question wording with clinical terms to find dedicated mental-health pages explaining why a person may feel watched."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"why do i feel like\" \"being watched\" (+ paranoia anxiety hypervigilance))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_381218626#0_968980398","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3751","rank":1,"snippet":"Whittome Is Taking A Leave Of Absence To Address PTSD By Alice Broster The word paranoia itself, as you might have guessed, comes from the ancient Greek. But when it shows up in ancient Greek tragedies by the likes of Euripides, it's used as a kind of synonym for \"crazy.\" The modern sense of heightened or excessive awareness of the harmful intentions of others didn't emerge until the 19th century, as German and French doctors refined the concept from a kind of delusion to a specific diagnosis. Paranoia also took on a new dimension in the 20th century as people began to study schizophrenia, which occasionally results in paranoia and hallucinations. But having a sense of paranoia doesn't necessarily mean you should see a mental health care professional, or that you're being haunted:\" , \"start_char\": 0.000000 , \"title\": \"Why Do I Feel Like I’m Being Watched? This Is The Scientific Reason For Paranoia\" , \"url\": \"https://www.bustle.com/p/<cover>why-do-i-feel-like-im-being-watched-this-is-the-scientific-reason-for-paranoia-</cover>2920450\" }"}}
