{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_381218626#0_968980398","relevance":3,"reason":"Dedicated article with the exact question in the title and a scientific/paranoia framing; the snippet explains paranoia as heightened or excessive awareness of others' harmful intentions, directly supporting why someone may feel watched.","learned":"A key explanation is paranoia: excessive threat awareness or suspicion of others' harmful intentions can produce the feeling of being watched, though paranoia exists on a spectrum and is not always a disorder."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3751","reason":"This query retrieved a highly focused, dedicated article; continuing may surface more segments or distinct pages with direct explanations of feeling watched."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3751"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_381218626#1_968982949","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3751","rank":2,"snippet":"a sense of paranoia doesn't necessarily mean you should see a mental health care professional, or that you're being haunted: the idea of being wary of others in your environment seems to have developed as a pretty natural part of our survival. Normal Paranoia Likely Developed To Help Us Survive GarbageVEVO on YouTube Paranoia, it's suggested, likely evolved as an evolutionary strategy. \" Wariness of the intentions of others may be adaptive in some situations ,\" noted the British Journal of Psychiatry in 2013, \"and becomes a clinical problem only when it is excessive, exaggerated or distressing, or interferes with functioning.\" So what's the story behind its development under normal circumstances? The dominant theory is that we experience paranoia because our nervous systems and brains are designed to assess and highlight something called \"social threat\":\" , \"start_char\": 914.000000 , \"title\": \"Why Do I Feel Like I’m Being Watched? This Is The Scientific Reason For Paranoia\" , \"url\": \"https://www.bustle.com/p/<cover>why-do-i-feel-like-im-being-watched-this-is-the-scientific-reason-for-paranoia-</cover>2920450\" }"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"12/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_381218626#1_968982949","relevance":3,"reason":"Dedicated article answering the exact question; this segment directly explains that feeling watched/paranoia may be an evolved survival strategy involving wariness of others and brain/nervous-system assessment of social threat.","learned":"Normal paranoia may have evolved to aid survival: the brain and nervous system assess social threat, and this adaptive wariness becomes problematic when excessive or distressing."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3751","reason":"Continue through this highly relevant result list to collect more direct supporting evidence from the same dedicated article or other focused documents."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3751"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_381218626#4_968989572","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3751","rank":3,"snippet":"were almost as common. A sixth of the population spent a lot of time wondering whether they could trust their friends or work colleagues.\" Most people would regard these as pretty average fears and concerns about the intentions and thoughts of others. As paranoid ideas became more extreme — worries that friends were actively watching them, beliefs that strangers were talking about them, and finally the unshakeable conviction that known or unknown forces were out to hurt them — the number of people who ticked \"yes\" got smaller and smaller. In those people, evolutionary strategy had given way to a more serious problem. If you feel like your paranoia has gotten in the way of your day-to-day life, you should speak to a mental health care professional or primary care physician about potential ways to manage it.\" , \"start_char\": 3410.000000 , \"title\": \"Why Do I Feel Like I’m Being Watched? This Is The Scientific Reason For Paranoia\" , \"url\": \"https://www.bustle.com/p/<cover>why-do-i-feel-like-im-being-watched-this-is-the-scientific-reason-for-paranoia-</cover>2920450\" }"}}
