{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_06_309441366#3_541142322","relevance":3,"reason":"Dedicated article on fear of being watched/scopophobia; this segment directly explains excessive fear of being watched or stared at, links it to autism and schizophrenia, and describes avoidance and panic-like responses to the thought of being watched.","learned":"In extreme scopophobia, the fear of being watched becomes disproportionate and may be associated with autism or schizophrenia; sufferers may avoid public situations and want to flee or hide when they think they are being watched."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ hypervigilance \"being watched\" (+ trauma ptsd anxiety))","reason":"Search a distinct high-yield explanation: trauma/PTSD-related hypervigilance can make people feel watched or constantly monitored for threat."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ hypervigilance \"being watched\" (+ trauma ptsd anxiety))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1601945736#4_3353439309","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3763","rank":1,"snippet":"\" , \"segment\": \"Stress hormones such as cortisol and norepinephrine flood the body, which prepares to defend itself. During this time, our body automatically tenses up. The problem is that tension doesn’t always dissipate once the threat is removed or neutralized. In this case, the brain’s neural networks will maintain a state of <cover>hypervigilance, acting as if the threat remains and preparing the body accordingly. 2. Social withdrawal Research demonstrates that childhood trauma contributes to Social Anxiety Disorder or SAD. Parental abuse (e.g., denigrating, insulting, swearing, verbal aggression) and emotional neglect (i.e., not feeling cared for, loved, or nurtured) relate directly to the onset of SAD. Victims of childhood trauma who demonstrate social withdrawal often do so out of anxiety and fear. SAD patients report feeling “an intense, persistent fear of being watched </cover>and judged by others,” and may isolate themselves to prevent this feeling. 3.\" , \"start_char\": 1778.000000 , \"title\": \"5 Adult Behaviors of Someone That Experienced Trauma As A Child\" , \"url\": \"https://www.powerofpositivity.com/5-adult-behaviors-someone-experienced-trauma-child/"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"29/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1601945736#4_3353439309","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage gives a relevant causal explanation: trauma can leave the brain in hypervigilance, and childhood trauma may contribute to social anxiety with intense fear of being watched and judged. However, the article is broadly about adult behaviors after childhood trauma rather than dedicated to why people feel watched.","learned":"Trauma may produce hypervigilance, in which the brain/body continue acting as if threat remains; trauma-related social anxiety can include fear of being watched and judged."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"feeling watched\" (+ hypervigilance ptsd trauma anxiety))","reason":"Search for a more dedicated trauma/PTSD or hypervigilance source explicitly connecting the feeling of being watched to threat monitoring."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"feeling watched\" (+ hypervigilance ptsd trauma anxiety))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_09_769569898#3_1296717876","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3764","rank":1,"snippet":"forms – physical, emotional, mental, psychic, environmental and life. Lets break them down for a closer look… Physical Feeling chronically tired, drained and depleted of energy Sudden onset of aches and pains in the body Scratches, bruises and marks on the body with no seeming cause Peculiar sensations such as pains, buzzing, crawling or tingling sensations Sleep paralysis Emotional Bursts of negative emotion such as <cover>anxiety, fear, guilt or panic attacks Low mood, depression or persistent sadness Relationship conflicts – uncharacteristically fighting, arguing A gut feeling that something isn’t right Mental Brain fog – inability to focus, feeling confused or dazed Disturbing nightmares or persistent images of faces Hearing voices or an inner negative self talk and criticism Repeating patterns of self sabotage or negative behaviors Feeling detached, disassociated and distant Psychic Sensing another presence or feeling watched</cover>Seeing lights, orbs, mists, shadows, figures or silhouettes Feeling that you are being touched Environmental Surroundings Cold areas in your home Pets growling or looking at something that you can’t see Noises – tapping, knocking, footsteps or voices Bad smells that can’t be located – e.g body odour, cigarette smoke Electronic items glitching, malfunctioning or breaking Losing items that show up later Life"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"30/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_09_769569898#3_1296717876","relevance":0,"reason":"This is a paranormal/psychic-presence list that mentions 'feeling watched' as a psychic sign, not a trustworthy psychological, medical, or scientific explanation.","learned":"The exact phrase 'feeling watched' with anxiety/trauma terms can still retrieve paranormal content; future queries should use clinical concepts such as paranoia, persecutory delusions, social anxiety, or hypervigilance more tightly."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"being watched\" paranoia \"mental health\" (+ delusions anxiety psychosis))","reason":"Search for clinically framed mental-health explanations of the feeling of being watched, while avoiding paranormal interpretations."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"being watched\" paranoia \"mental health\" (+ delusions anxiety psychosis))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_41_1689181162#1_2614794693","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3765","rank":1,"snippet":"for Anxiety About Being Watched References: Tonycliftontaxi Jonh Jo \" , \"segment\": \"Drawing the line between normal fears, anxiety, and paranoia can be difficult. That’s especially true when a person’s apparently paranoid fears turn out to be true—as was the case for activists targeted by programs such as COINTELPRO, and for Ernest Hemingway, who really did have an FBI file. Knowing where to draw that line, and how to decide whether a fear is reasonable or not, can help people seek appropriate <cover>mental health care. What Is Paranoia? Paranoia is persistent anxiety about a specific fear. Paranoid anxieties often center around persecution, being watched, </cover>or being treated unjustly. The hallmark of paranoia is that it is rooted in a false belief. People with paranoid thoughts may also have false beliefs about their own power or importance. For example, a person who does not occupy a political position or engage in activism might believe in an international conspiracy to monitor and torture them. In some cases, exposure to trauma or severe stress can make people more likely to experience paranoia.\" , \"start_char\": 551.000000 , \"title"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"31/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_41_1689181162#1_2614794693","relevance":3,"reason":"Dedicated mental-health article about anxiety/paranoia involving being watched; it directly explains that paranoia is persistent anxiety centered on persecution or being watched, rooted in false belief, and that trauma or severe stress can increase paranoia risk.","learned":"Feeling watched can be a form of paranoia: persistent anxiety about persecution or monitoring, often based in false beliefs; trauma or severe stress can make paranoia more likely."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3765","reason":"This clinically framed query is producing dedicated, directly relevant mental-health evidence about being watched, anxiety, paranoia, delusions, and stress/trauma; continue for more grade-3 results."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3765"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_41_1689181162#12_2614812616","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3765","rank":2,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_41_1689181162#12_2614812616\" , \"end_char\": 6914.000000 , \"headings\": \"What Is the Difference Between Paranoia and Reasonable Suspicion? What Is the Difference Between Paranoia and Reasonable Suspicion? What Is Paranoia? Delusional vs. Reasonable Paranoia Schizophrenia Symptoms: The Link to <cover>Paranoia and Anxiety When to Seek Treatment for Anxiety About Being Watched References: Tonycliftontaxi Jonh Jo \" , \"segment\": \"A person with schizophrenia might try to contact a celebrity to warn them of a threat or reach out to a lawyer to report government surveillance that doesn’t seem to be happening. When to Seek Treatment for Anxiety About Being Watched Mental health </cover>treatment can help anyone experiencing anxiety, whether their anxiety is rooted in a real source or the product of a mental health diagnosis. Activists such as lawyers or protest leaders who have reasonable fears about being monitored may find therapy helps them manage their anxiety, deal with the effects of those fears on their relationships, and separate reasonable fears from unreasonable ones. People who have a condition linked to unreasonable paranoia may "}}
