{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_41_1689181162#11_2614810810","relevance":3,"reason":"Dedicated mental-health article about anxiety/paranoia over being watched; this segment directly links anxiety about being watched to false beliefs, schizophrenia-related behavior such as unfounded government-surveillance concerns, and the role of mental-health treatment.","learned":"Anxiety about being watched can stem from real concerns or from mental-health conditions; when tied to false beliefs or schizophrenia-like symptoms, treatment can help manage the anxiety and reality-test the fear."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3762","reason":"The scopophobia query has been the highest-yield source of distinct dedicated grade-3 evidence explaining fear of being watched or stared at, so continuing it is likely to uncover more directly relevant results."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3762"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_26_726335721#2_1380107133","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3762","rank":7,"snippet":"000000 , \"headings\": \"Scopophobia – Fear of Being Stared At Scopophobia – Fear of Being Stared At What Is Scopophobia? What Are the Symptoms of Scopophobia? What Causes Scopophobia? Genetics Early negative experiences Physical deformity Neurological conditions How to Manage Scopophobia? Systematic desensitization Cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT) Hypnotherapy Meditation How to reference this article: \" , \"segment\": \"The term scopophobia was first mentioned in 1906 in the psychiatric journal The Alienist and Neurologist, a few years after French psychiatrist Pierre Janet described the irrational <cover>fear of being watched in some of his patients. Scopophobia is a social phobia or social anxiety </cover>disorder triggered by interpersonal interactions. The anxiety is made worse in social situations involving large crowds such as public speaking. People with scopophobia are often not only terrified by the looks of strangers but also people they know well, including friends and family members. What is more, the fear is present even when the attention of others is not negative. While having some amount of social anxiety is not unusual, the fear related to scopophobia is disproportional compared to the actual risk. A mere thought of being looked at can be enough "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"35/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_26_726335721#2_1380107133","relevance":3,"reason":"Dedicated article on scopophobia/fear of being stared at; it directly explains fear of being watched as a social phobia/social anxiety disorder triggered by interpersonal interactions, worsened in crowds or public speaking, and disproportionate to actual risk.","learned":"Scopophobia is a social anxiety-related fear of being watched; it can be triggered by interpersonal attention, crowds, or public speaking, and may occur even when others' attention is not negative."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3762","reason":"The scopophobia result list is still yielding dedicated, directly relevant explanations of fear of being watched, so continuing is likely to find more grade-3 evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3762"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_26_726335721#1_1380105469","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3762","rank":8,"snippet":"this article: \" , \"segment\": \"She is convinced that people are observing her and judging her awkwardness while in reality, nobody notices. This type of anxiety is called scopophobia. What Is Scopophobia? Scopophobia is a fear of any sort of visual attention, such as being looked at or being photographed, for example. Other names for scopophobia are scoptophobia and ophthalmophobia. The term scopophobia was first mentioned in 1906 in the psychiatric journal The Alienist and Neurologist, a few years after French psychiatrist Pierre Janet described the irrational <cover>fear of being watched in some of his patients. Scopophobia is a social phobia or social anxiety </cover>disorder triggered by interpersonal interactions. The anxiety is made worse in social situations involving large crowds such as public speaking. People with scopophobia are often not only terrified by the looks of strangers but also people they know well, including friends and family members. What is more, the fear is present even when the attention of others is not negative.\" , \"start_char\": 520.000000 , \"title\": \"Scopophobia - Fear of Being Stared At - Practical Psychology\" , \"url\": \""}}
{"request":{"judgments":"36/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_26_726335721#1_1380105469","relevance":3,"reason":"Dedicated article on scopophobia/fear of being stared at; it directly explains the feeling as fear of visual attention and a social phobia/social anxiety disorder triggered by interpersonal interactions, including feeling observed or judged even when others do not notice.","learned":"Scopophobia involves fear of visual attention such as being looked at, watched, or photographed; it can make people feel observed and judged even without negative attention, and is related to social anxiety."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"being watched\" \"persecutory delusions\" (+ paranoia psychosis schizophrenia))","reason":"Search for another clinical explanation of feeling watched: persecutory delusions/paranoia in psychosis or schizophrenia, likely to yield dedicated mental-health evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"being watched\" \"persecutory delusions\" (+ paranoia psychosis schizophrenia))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_26_1026574528#5_2018393674","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3767","rank":1,"snippet":"D-aspartate … \" , \"segment\": \"Somatic passivity) Have you felt that people on the radio or TV. were talking about you in their reports, or giving you special messages? ( Ideas or Delusions of Reference) Do you ever receive any special messages from books, radio or TV that are specifically meant for you? ( Paranoid bizarre delusions) Do you ever feel like you are being spied upon like someone is following you or conspiring against you? ( Persecutory Delusions) Do you ever feel like you are <cover>being watched by cameras or special devices? ( Persecutory Delusions) How did it start? ( To establish delusional perception) YouTube PsychScene Hub 9.24K subscribers Subscribe Understanding Schizophrenia - </cover>Linking Neurobiology to Clinical Symptoms Info Shopping Tap to unmute If playback doesn't begin shortly, try restarting your device. More videos More videos You're signed out Videos you watch may be added to the TV's watch history and influence TV recommendations. To avoid this, cancel and sign in to YouTube on your computer. Cancel Confirm Switch camera Share Include playlist An error occurred while retrieving sharing information.\" , \"start_char\": 1803.000000 , \"title\": \""}}
