{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"being watched\" \"social threat\" (+ paranoia evolutionary psychology))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_381218626#2_968985311","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3770","rank":2,"snippet":"3410.000000 , \"headings\": \"If You Feel Like You’re Always Being Watched, There’s A Scientific Reason Why If You Feel Like You’re Always <cover>Being Watched, There’s A Scientific Reason Why Normal Paranoia Likely Developed To Help Us Survive \" , \"segment\": \"the idea of being wary of others in your environment seems to have developed as a pretty natural part of our survival. Normal Paranoia Likely Developed To Help Us Survive GarbageVEVO on YouTube Paranoia, it's suggested, likely evolved as an evolutionary strategy. \\\" Wariness of the intentions of others may be adaptive in some situations ,\\\" noted the British Journal of Psychiatry in 2013, \\\"and becomes a clinical problem only when it is excessive, exaggerated or distressing, or interferes with functioning.\\\" So what's the story behind its development under normal circumstances? The dominant theory is that we experience paranoia because our nervous systems and brains are designed to assess and highlight something called \\\"social threat\\\": </cover>the potential signals being given by other people that they may be hostile or not have our best interests at heart. A review of paranoia science in 2004 found that we ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_381218626#1_968982949","rank":1,"relevance":3}]}}
