{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_12_1624726350#10_3239930988","relevance":3,"reason":"Dedicated science article on why people can sense someone staring; it directly explains the feeling through gaze detection, threat/dominance signaling, the brain's bias toward assuming gaze to avoid missing danger, and a possible self-fulfilling prophecy when turning around draws another person's gaze.","learned":"People may feel watched because direct gaze can signal threat or dominance, so the brain may use a cautious strategy of assuming someone is looking; turning around can also draw someone’s gaze and make it seem they had been staring all along."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3772","reason":"This query found a dedicated scientific explanation of the watched/stared-at feeling via gaze detection, likely to produce more grade-3 evidence from the same or related sources."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3772"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_12_1624726350#9_3239929456","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3772","rank":2,"snippet":"someone is starting at us when we can't see them, even if we have no evidence to suggest so. We're hardwired to assume someone is staring from behind. Getty Images. Psychology Professor Colin Clifford of the University of Sydney's Vision Centre, found that when people can't tell where a person is looking, they automatically assume they're looking at them. “ A direct <cover>gaze can signal dominance or a threat, and if you perceive something as a threat, you would not want to miss it,\" he said. “ So simply assuming another person is looking at you may be the safest strategy.\" Looking at someone is also a social cue. It usually means you want to talk to them. Since it's our natural inclination to assume someone behind us is staring, the feeling we get may initiate a self-fulfilling prophecy.\" , \"start_char\": 3361.000000 , \"title\": \"Why Is It You Can Sense When Someone’</cover>s Staring at You? - Big Think\" , \"url\": \"https://bigthink.com/philip-perry/why-is-it-you-can-sense-when-someones-staring-at-you\" }"}}
