{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_12_1624726350#9_3239929456","relevance":3,"reason":"Dedicated science article on why people sense someone staring; it directly explains that when gaze direction is ambiguous, people are biased to assume they are being looked at because direct gaze can signal dominance or threat, making the assumption a safer strategy.","learned":"When people cannot tell where someone is looking, the brain may default to assuming the gaze is directed at them; this threat-avoidance bias can create the feeling of being watched."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3772","reason":"The current query is producing dedicated scientific explanations of the watched/stared-at feeling via gaze detection and threat bias; continue to collect more direct evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3772"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_55_273139816#10_627938887","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3772","rank":3,"snippet":"your direction. It's also possible that you subconsciously noticed that person staring at you before you turned your back, and your memory alerted you to the fact several minutes later. … but that can actually be a good thing. Let's face it, we humans are self-centered beasts — beasts that once upon a time had to survive a far greater risk of violent threats than we do now. We're sensitive to gaze, researchers believe, because that sensitivity can be a tool for survival. \" Direct <cover>gaze can signal dominance or a threat, and if you perceive something as a threat, you would not want to miss it,\" Colin Clifford, a cognitive scientist at the University of New South Wales and one of the co-authors of the Current Biology study, explained in a statement. \" So assuming that the other person is looking at you may simply be a safer strategy.\"\" , \"start_char\": 5346.000000 , \"title\": \"The scientific reasons you can 'sense' when someone </cover>is watching you\" , \"url\": \"https://www.theweek.com/articles/714172/scientific-reasons-sense-when-someone-watching\" }"}}
